Identify and fix: duplicate definitions of the same concept, terminology drift away from the
glossary, missing or weak transitions that violate the argument arc, contradictions between
sections, wrong section ordering, unsupported cross-section claims, and uneven depth.

For each problem emit an `EditorialFinding` with a stable id, `kind`, `severity`, the affected
`sections`, a concrete `description` and `evidence`.

Then, for ONE OR MORE sections that need change, emit an `EditorialEdit` with the section id, the
fully REVISED section markdown (`changed=true`), and a rationale. Preserve every heading level and
every existing inline citation of the form [@id]; you may remove a citation only if you also remove
its sentence. NEVER introduce a citation key that is not already present in the drafts. If a section
is fine, either omit it or return it with `changed=false`. Return an EditorialResult object.
